%!testlib
% The page device accumulates its parameters, run through
% `xpost -q --no-sandbox -d null` by run-ps-test.sh.
%
% setpagedevice merges each request into the page-device dictionary the
% last call left, and currentpagedevice reports it, so a program that
% names a fresh key on every call grows that dictionary without end. The
% whole of it is copied on every call, to keep a restore able to put the
% previous one back, so an unbounded dictionary makes an unbounded copy
% on every page -- the cost of a run the square of its calls. The count
% of accumulated keys is bounded well above the standard parameters and
% any device's own, so a request naming that many distinct keys is
% refused (limitcheck) rather than a run made to grow without end.

% A rich request naming every standard parameter at once is far below the
% bound and is accepted.
{ << /PageSize [612 792] /HWResolution [72 72] /ImagingBBox null
     /Orientation 0 /NumCopies 1 /MediaColor (white) /MediaType (plain)
     /Duplex false /Tumble false /Collate false /BeginPage {} /EndPage {}
     /Install {} /Policies << /PageSize 3 >> >> setpagedevice }
    (a request naming the standard parameters is accepted) works

% The same keys set again and again do not accumulate: each put replaces a
% key already there, so the count does not move. Six hundred such requests
% -- more than the bound -- prove it by not reaching the bound; were the
% keys accumulating, this many would cross it and raise.
{ 1 1 600 { pop << /PageSize [1 1] /NumCopies 1 >> setpagedevice } for }
    (repeated identical requests do not accumulate to the bound) works

% A program naming a distinct key on every call crosses the bound and is
% refused, rather than growing the accumulated dictionary without end.
{ 1 1 700 { /i exch def
    << /PageSize [1 1] i 20 string cvs cvn i >> setpagedevice } for }
    /limitcheck (naming a fresh key on every call is refused) raises

verdict
